# Extra GPU memory does not scale linearly with sequence length under eager attention

This project's model (`transformers`' Qwen3 loaded without an explicit
`attn_implementation` override) uses **eager attention**, whose memory cost
is quadratic in the flattened sequence length (`max_flat_len`), not linear.
An 80GB H100 has ~2.5x a 32GB V100's memory, but that only buys roughly
`sqrt(2.5)` ≈ 1.6x more safe sequence length at the same batch size, not
2.5x -- and batch_size and max_flat_len multiply together, so increasing
both at once compounds fast.

## What happened (2026-08-04)

A newly-launched H100 search widened `batch_size_choices` to `[2,4,6,8]`
and `max_flat_len_choices` to `[3072,4096,6144]`, and only forced
`gradient_checkpointing` at `batch_size>=8` (reasoning from raw GPU memory
headroom alone). Result: **22 of the first 22 real candidates OOM'd**,
including the *smallest* new combination tried
(`batch_size=2, max_flat_len=3072, no checkpointing`).

## 2nd correction, same day: checkpointing is not always enough

Forcing `gradient_checkpointing=True` fixed the 22/22 failure rate above,
but `batch_size=8, max_flat_len=4096` (checkpointed) still intermittently
OOM'd -- one candidate completed ~500 steps, an identically-configured one
OOM'd at step ~456 with only 296MiB free out of 79.18GB. Checkpointing
avoids retaining per-layer activations across the whole forward pass, but
does not lower a combination's *steady-state* working set below ~99.6% GPU
utilization -- there was never real margin for normal run-to-run variance
(different batches have different actual token counts within the
`max_flat_len` cap). Fix: cap `max_flat_len` at 2048 once `batch_size`
reaches 8, even with checkpointing already forced on. **One candidate
succeeding is not sufficient evidence a combination is safe** -- prefer
observing several successful full-length runs (not just one smoke test)
before trusting a combination that's already close to the memory ceiling.

## Fix and standing lesson

- Any `max_flat_len` beyond the previously-validated ~2048 ceiling, or any
  `batch_size >= 4`, must force `gradient_checkpointing=True` on this
  model regardless of how much raw GPU memory is available -- checkpointing
  avoids retaining the O(seq_len^2) attention-weight tensor across the
  whole forward pass, which is the actual memory bottleneck, not raw
  parameter/activation storage.
- **Before trusting a widened search space with real compute, smoke-test
  the riskiest single combination directly** (a short, ~5-step `main.py`
  invocation is enough) rather than reasoning from memory math alone --
  eager attention's actual behavior is not intuitive from "GPU has more
  RAM" logic.
- If a long-lived controller process already has bad values baked into
  the ON-DISK `search_state.json`'s `config` (not just the `--config`
  file passed at launch), restarting the controller does NOT pick up a
  corrected config file: `read_json(paths.state) or initial_state(config)`
  only calls `initial_state` (which would read the fresh config) if the
  state file doesn't exist yet. If it already exists, the freshly-loaded
  `--config` is silently ignored. Patch `search_state.json`'s embedded
  `config` dict directly in this case.
- Also check for a stale `controller.lock`/`watchdog.lock` (and fresh-looking
  `controller_lease.json` heartbeat) left behind by a process you just
  killed -- restarting within the lease window (default 600s) will make the
  new process think another instance is still alive and refuse to start.
  Release the lock directory manually if you're certain the old process is
  dead.
